To qualify generally for CAP/DA services, a beneficiary must meet NC Medicaid’s CAP/DA coverage criteria, including nursing-facility level-of-care needs and the ability to remain safely in a private residence with supports. In particular, NC Medicaid covers CAP/DA when the beneficiary:

- meets the required HCBS nursing facility level of care, based on a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
- has a CAP assessment showing a reasonable indication they will need at least one CAP/DA HCBS service within 30 calendar days of the evaluation;
- has a signed service plan authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis, or a short-term installation/modification/technology service to help the person return to or remain in the home and avoid institutional placement;
- can maintain health, safety, and well-being in the primary private residence or other approved location using formal and informal supports;
- has paid care needs that can be maintained within the average institutional cost of the CAP/DA waiver;
- has a primary physician who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- has an emergency back-up and disaster plan for situations when regular caregivers are unavailable or during public emergencies. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 3.2.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current per amendment check).

A few general “not covered” rules also help define eligibility boundaries. CAP/DA is not covered if the beneficiary does not meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0 or the criteria in Section 3.0, or if the requested service duplicates another provider’s service or is experimental/investigational. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 4.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current per amendment check).

The policy also lists examples of when CAP/DA cannot be covered, including when:
- the CAP special indicator code is missing from NC FAST and cannot be reentered;
- the HCBS Service Request Form is incomplete or denied;
- the required annual recertification was not approved or completed within 12 months;
- no medical/functional assessment was completed to determine reasonable indication of need; or
- the person’s health and safety cannot be met through the service plan because of an unsafe home environment or refusal to cooperate with the plan of care. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 4.2.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current per amendment check).

If you want, I can also give you a shorter intake-style checklist version of CAP/DA eligibility.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must have a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the plan period;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the plan’s start date**.  
  [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 3.2.1.6 (effective 2/1/2025; current per amendment check)

In addition, the policy’s documentation section requires the provider record to include **a copy of the treatment plan**. It also requires service notes for each billed date showing the skilled intervention, outcome/beneficiary response, duration in minutes, and the provider’s signature and credentials.  
[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 7.2 (effective 2/1/2025; current per amendment check)

If helpful, I can also pull the related **physician/order requirement** in 10A and explain how the treatment plan differs from the order and from prior authorization documentation.

Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must be certified by meeting the provider standards in Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, and the provider can be decertified for failing those standards or for specified compliance, billing, staffing, documentation, or legal violations.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid’s HIV Case Management policy requires the provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of 3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers**;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet all applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- comply with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- show compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records and inspect agency operations and financial records**; and
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days** and no later than **5 business days after the actual change**. [[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)]

In addition, **within 1 year of enrollment with Medicaid**, the provider must obtain **national accreditation** from one of the policy’s designated accrediting agencies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. [[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)]

### Related application/recertification requirements

The policy materials and recertification checklist also show that providers must document and maintain operational capacity, including items such as:

- all case managers and supervisors with dates of employment;
- all counties where HIV CM services are provided;
- services offered;
- operating hours and emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction survey frequency and use of results; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [[dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)]

The policy’s administrative and human-resource requirements also require, among other things:

- a **physical business site** verified by site visit;
- organizational chart;
- ownership disclosures for persons with 5% or more ownership;
- access to required web-based applications;
- a business plan and financial information;
- computer capability sufficient for electronic records, HIPAA compliance, data analysis, electronic billing, EFT, and monthly communication with CCNC or the primary care provider;
- completion of a **precertification onsite visit**;
- written policies on confidentiality, grievances, beneficiary rights, non-discrimination, ethics, conflict of interest, electronic and medical records, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation; and
- qualified staff and owners without disqualifying felony histories. [[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)]

## When a provider can be decertified

Policy 12B says an HIV case management provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony charge**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** within **30 calendar days** to resolve unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the qualifications in the policy;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days** of employment;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within policy or agency time frames;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid/DHHS contractor of agency changes, causing inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, state reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in section 6.2. [[Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)]

## Practical summary

In short, certification requires an HIV case management provider to show experience, qualified staffing and supervision, proper Medicaid enrollment of each site, accreditation, quality-improvement and documentation systems, operational readiness, and compliance with Medicaid rules. Decertification can occur if the provider falls out of compliance with those standards, especially around billing integrity, staffing qualifications, training, documentation, confidentiality, corrective action, and legal compliance. 

